.\" mailplate - apply a mailplate profile to an RFC5322 email draft
.\"
.TH MAILPLATE 1 "2026-10-12" "mailplate" "User Commands"
.\"
.SH NAME
mailplate \- apply a mailplate profile to an RFC5322 email draft
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailplate
[\fB\-p\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR \fIDIR\fR]...
[\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mailplate
is a Unix\-style filter for RFC5322 email drafts.
It reads a draft from standard input, rewrites it according to a
profile, and writes the result to standard output.
.PP
The profile controls which headers are added or overridden, how the
body is restructured (greeting, closing, signature blocks), and all
sections can contain template placeholders that are filled with data
extracted from the incoming message.
.PP
Only plain\-text drafts are supported: a message containing anything
other than
.B text/plain
(for example an HTML part or an attachment) is rejected with an error.
The output is a bare RFC 5322 message without MIME headers.
Non\-ASCII characters in header bodies are written verbatim as UTF\-8;
.B mailplate
does not RFC 2047\-encode them.
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " " \fINAME\fR
Apply the profile identified by \fINAME\fR (the filename stem, the path
relative to the scanned directory for profiles in subdirectories, or
the display name). When this option is omitted,
.B mailplate
auto\-detects the best matching profile by evaluating each profile's
rules against the message and selecting the one with the most matches.
A profile with no rules is a catch\-all with score 0.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print diagnostic information to standard error, including the profile
search paths, discovery counts, per\-rule match details, and the
selection rationale.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-profile-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Add \fIDIR\fR to the list of directories scanned for profile files.
May be specified more than once. Extra directories take precedence over
the default search paths. Directories are scanned recursively, so
profiles in subdirectories are found as well.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the transformed message to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list-profiles
List all available profiles and exit. Each line shows the identifier
(the filename stem, or the path relative to the scanned directory for
profiles in subdirectories, used with \fB\-p\fR) and the display name.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.
.\"
.SH PROFILE FORMAT
Profiles are flat text files with a
.B .mplt
extension. The format is INI\-like with sections delimited by
.BR [square\ brackets] .
Comments start with
.B #
and blank lines are ignored, except inside
.BR [signature] ,
whose content is preserved verbatim.
.PP
.IP \fB[profile]\fR 18
Profile metadata. The only recognised field is
.BR name .
If absent, the filename stem is used as the display name.
.IP \fB[rules]\fR 18
Auto\-detection rules. Each line has the form
.RS
.IP \[bu]
\fIHeaderName\fR = \fIregex\fR
.IP \[bu]
\fIHeaderName\fR:\fIweight\fR = \fIregex\fR
.IP \[bu]
\fBbody\fR = \fIregex\fR
.IP \[bu]
\fBbody\fR:\fIweight\fR = \fIregex\fR
.IP \[bu]
\fBrcpt\fR = \fIregex\fR
.IP \[bu]
\fBrcpt\fR:\fIweight\fR = \fIregex\fR
.RE
.IP
The optional
.I weight
defaults to 1 and influences auto\-detection scoring. It may be zero,
in which case the rule still contributes its named captures but adds
nothing to the score, or negative, in which case it subtracts points
and a sufficiently negative rule can stop its profile from being
selected. Field names are matched case\-insensitively. The field name
.B body
is reserved for the body rule; the field name
.B rcpt
matches against all of the
.BR To ,
.BR Cc ,
and
.B Bcc
headers at once (the rule applies if the regexp matches any of them).
The old
.B header =
form is no longer accepted.
.IP
Each rule is matched independently: every matching rule adds its weight
to the profile's score, while a non\-matching rule adds nothing. The
profile with the highest total score wins, provided that score is at
least 1.
.IP
Named capture groups in a rule's regexp become template variables in
the
.B rule:
namespace. For example, the rule
.RS
.IP
.B From = .*@(?P<domain>[a-z0-9.]+)
.RE
.IP
makes
.B {{ rule:domain }}
available in the
.BR [headers] ,
.BR [greeting] ,
.BR [closing] ,
and
.B [signature]
sections. Rules are evaluated for named captures both during
auto\-detection and when a profile is selected explicitly with
.BR \-\-profile .
.IP \fB[headers]\fR 18
Header definitions in the form
.BI "Header-Name = " value.
Existing headers with the same name (case\-insensitive) are replaced.
Template placeholders are expanded in header values, so a value may
reference other headers via
.B {{ header:<Name> }}
as well as the shorthand variables listed below.
.IP \fB[greeting]\fR 18
Lines inserted before the original body. A blank line after the
greeting is provided automatically. Template variables are expanded.
.IP \fB[closing]\fR 18
Lines inserted after the original body but before the signature.
Template variables are expanded.
.IP \fB[signature]\fR 18
Lines appended at the end of the message, separated from the closing
by a
.B "-- "
delimiter line. Template variables are expanded. Signature lines are
copied verbatim: leading whitespace and blank lines are preserved
exactly as written. If the original draft already contains a
signature (text following a
.B "-- "
line), it is replaced by this section; if no
.B [signature]
section is defined, the original signature is preserved unchanged.
.IP \fB[remove_headers]\fR 18
One header name per line. Matching headers (case\-insensitive) are
deleted from the output, after
.B [headers]
have been applied.
.IP \fB[helpers]\fR 18
External command definitions in the form
.BI "name = " "shell command" .
The command is split with
.B shlex.split
and run without a shell, with a five\-second timeout. Its standard
output can be inserted with
.B {{ helper:<name> }}
in any template section. For example:
.RS
.IP
.nf
[helpers]
get_quote     = fortune \-n2
get_long_date = date '+\&%d \&%B \&%Y'
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "INCLUDE files"
.br
A file named exactly
.B INCLUDE
(no extension) in a profile directory is applied automatically to every
profile in that directory and its subdirectories. When
.I foo/bar/prof.mplt
is loaded, the root\-level
.BR INCLUDE ,
.IR foo/INCLUDE ,
and
.I foo/bar/INCLUDE
are applied in that order (least specific first), followed by the
profile itself. An
.B INCLUDE
file uses the same format as a profile but must not contain a
.B [profile]
section.
.PP
The
.BR [rules] ,
.BR [headers] ,
.B [helpers] ,
and
.B [remove_headers]
sections accumulate across all files, with the more specific file
winning on a name collision; verbatim duplicate rules are kept once.
The
.BR [greeting] ,
.BR [closing] ,
and
.B [signature]
sections are single\-valued: the most specific file that defines the
section wins, and an explicitly empty section clears an inherited one.
.PP
.I Idempotency
.br
If the body already starts with the rendered greeting and/or ends with
the rendered closing+signature block, those are stripped before the
fresh decorations are applied. Running
.B mailplate
multiple times with the same profile therefore does not keep piling on
extra greeting or closing lines.
.\"
.SH TEMPLATE VARIABLES
The following placeholders can be used in the
.BR [headers] ,
.BR [greeting] ,
.BR [closing] ,
and
.B [signature]
sections.
.PP
.TS
l lx.
\fBVariable\fR	\fBD e s c r i p t i o n\fR
_
{{ body }}	Original plain\-text body of the message.
{{ from }}	Full \fBFrom\fR header value.
{{ from_name }}	Display\-name portion of the \fBFrom\fR address.
{{ from_email }}	Email\-address portion of the \fBFrom\fR address.
{{ to }}	Full \fBTo\fR header value.
{{ to_name }}	Display\-name of the first \fBTo\fR address.
{{ to_email }}	Email of the first \fBTo\fR address.
{{ subject }}	\fBSubject\fR header value.
{{ date }}	\fBDate\fR header value.
{{ header:\fIname\fR }}	Value of an arbitrary header.
{{ env:\fIvar\fR }}	Value of an environment variable.
{{ helper:\fIname\fR }}	Output of a helper command from \fB[helpers]\fR.
{{ rule:\fIcapture\fR }}	Named regexp group from a matching rule.
.TE
.PP
The
.B default
filter supplies a fallback when a variable is empty:
.RS
.IP
{{ to_name | default(\(dqthere\(dq) }}
.RE
.\"
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mailplate/profiles/**/*.mplt
User\-specific profile files (found recursively).
.TP
.I /etc/mailplate/profiles/**/*.mplt
System\-wide profile files (found recursively).
.\"
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Apply the \fIwork\fR profile explicitly
.RS
mailplate \-\-profile work < draft.eml > rewritten.eml
.RE
.TP
Auto\-detect a profile from the input
.RS
mailplate < draft.eml > rewritten.eml
.RE
.TP
Edit a draft in\-place (via temporary output)
.RS
mailplate \-\-profile personal \-o draft.eml < draft.eml
.RE
.TP
List available profiles
.RS
mailplate \-\-list-profiles
.RE
.TP
Apply a profile from a subdirectory
.RS
mailplate \-\-profile work/foo < draft.eml > rewritten.eml
.RE
.TP
Print diagnostics to standard error
.RS
mailplate \-\-verbose < draft.eml > rewritten.eml
.RE
.\"
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Successful completion.
.TP
1
An error occurred (profile not found, no input data, stdin empty,
etc.).
.\"
